%% ============================================================================
%% SkyIntruders.pidoc — PixInsight reference documentation, English
%% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
%% Compile with the Documentation Compiler script. The generated page ships
%% in the update package as doc/scripts/SkyIntruders/SkyIntruders.html.
%% The French page lives in SkyIntruders-fr.pidoc; keep both in sync.
%% Special characters: write \% for a percent sign (% starts a comment), and
%% never write two consecutive hyphens (the compiler turns them into a dash).
%% ============================================================================

\documentclass PIScriptDoc

\script SkyIntruders

\keywords { satellite trails, satellite identification, TLE, SGP4, meteors, asteroids, deep-sky catalogs, plate solving }

\author { Caelo Works }

\copyright { 2026 Caelo Works }

\brief { Detects the trails that crossed a night of light frames and identifies
the satellites by name, and finds the catalogued objects hiding in a
plate-solved image. }

\description {

\xref ../SkyIntruders-fr/SkyIntruders-fr.html { Version française de cette documentation }

Sky Intruders works in two modes, one per tab of its dialog. Everything runs
inside PixInsight: there is no native helper to install.

\list [spaced] {

{ \s { Night trails } scans the light frames of one night. The frames are
registered internally and trails are detected on the registered difference
image, so nebulosity is not mistaken for a trail. Every trail is cross-matched
against orbital elements propagated with SGP4, so that an identified trail
carries the name of the satellite. Unmatched trails are tested against the
active meteor showers, and slow, coherent movers become asteroid candidates.
The result is an annotated composite image and a night log with a ready-to-post
forum text. }

{ \s { Treasure Hunt } takes one plate-solved image and queries deep catalogs
for the galaxies, quasars, planetary nebulae and asteroids inside the field.
Every catalog position is then measured on the image, so the report separates
the objects actually captured from those in the field but below the noise of
the image. The result is a star chart, opened as a new image window, and an
illustrated HTML report. }

}

Both modes download catalog data over HTTPS; see
\lref network { Network connections } for every host contacted and what is
sent to it, and \lref files { Files written on your computer } for everything
the script stores.

}

\usage {

The script is available from the \s { Script > CaeloWorks > Sky Intruders }
menu item. It requires PixInsight 1.9.4 or newer, on Windows, macOS and Linux.
On PixInsight 1.9.6 and newer, PNG support is a separate module: install it
once with \s { Process > Modules > Install Modules } . Without it, the
annotated night image is shown but not saved, and the Treasure Hunt report has
no pictures; the console, and the report, say so.

\subsection { Input list } {

The \s Input list is shared by both modes. \s { Add files… } accepts
\c { .fits }, \c { .fit }, \c { .fts } and \c { .xisf } files, \s { Add folder… }
adds the frames of one folder (it does not recurse into subfolders), and
\s Clear empties the list. A file already in the list is not added twice.

In \s { Night trails } the list holds the light frames of one night, which
need the \c DATE-OBS and \c EXPTIME keywords. The first frame of the list is
the registration reference and the source of the plate solution of the field:
put a plate-solved frame first. Satellite identification also needs an
observer site, read from the \c SITELAT / \c SITELONG, \c OBSGEO-B /
\c OBSGEO-L or \c LAT-OBS / \c LONG-OBS keywords, or entered by hand in the
dialog. Without a site, trails are still detected but cannot be named, and the
dialog says \s { No observer site — satellites not identified. }

In \s { Treasure Hunt } the list holds one plate-solved image. When the list
is empty, the active image window is used. An image without an astrometric
solution is refused with a message.

The \s Language list switches the interface, the reports and the star chart
between English and French. Every setting is remembered between sessions.
The documentation button at the left of the \s Language list opens this page,
in the interface language, in the PixInsight documentation browser.

}

\subsection { Night trails tab } {

\table [header] {
   { { Control } { Default } { Effect } }
   { { \s { Detection threshold (σ): } } { 4.5 } { Threshold, in robust
     sigmas, on the registered difference image used by every normal run of
     three frames or more. Range 2.5 to 8; a lower value catches fainter
     trails at the cost of false detections. } }
   { { \s { Draw predicted crossers on the result image } } { off } { Draws,
     as pale ghost lines, the satellites the orbit model places inside the
     field during an exposure but that no detected trail confirmed. } }
   { { \s { Also draw shadow crossers } } { off } { Same, for the satellites
     the model places in the shadow of the Earth, drawn in grey. } }
   { { \s { Observer site — only if the FITS headers carry none } } { empty }
     { \s { Lat (°, +N): } , \s { Lon (°, +E): } and \s { Alt (m): } . Used
     only when no frame carries a site. North and east are positive. Decimal
     values with a dot or a comma and sexagesimal values are accepted; an
     out-of-range or unreadable value is cleared and the status line says
     why. } }
   { { \s { Single-frame threshold (σ): } } { 5.0 } { In the \s Advanced
     group. Used only in the degraded per-frame path: fewer than three frames,
     or a set that could not be registered. Range 3 to 12. } }
   { { \s { Flag slow movers as asteroid candidates } } { on } { In the
     \s Advanced group. Unchecked, the slow-mover search is skipped. } }
   { { \s { Orbital data max age (h): } } { 12 } { In the \s Advanced group.
     Cached orbital elements older than this are downloaded again. Range 1 to
     168. } }
   { { \s { Orbital data mirror URL: } } { empty } { In the \s Advanced group.
     Base URL of a CelesTrak-compatible mirror, tried first when set. It must
     start with \c { https:// } ; any other value is cleared and the status
     line reads \s { Mirror URL must start with https:// — ignored. } }
     }
}

\s { Analyze night } runs the analysis. The result window shows the night
log; \s { Save report… } saves it to a file you choose, and \s { Open image }
opens the annotated night image with the default image viewer of your system.

}

\subsection { Treasure Hunt tab } {

\table [header] {
   { { Control } { Default } { Effect } }
   { { \s { Max catalog rows / type: } } { 400 } { Maximum number of objects
     fetched per catalog. Range 50 to 2000. Each catalog is asked to sort its
     answer, so the faintest or smallest objects are the ones dropped. } }
   { { \s { Hunt for: } } { all checked } { \s Galaxies , \s Quasars ,
     \s { Planetary nebulae } and \s Asteroids . An unchecked type is not
     queried. } }
   { { \s { Overlay color: } } { \c { &#35;9FD8D2 } } { Color of the markers,
     leader lines, labels and cards of the star chart. The swatch button opens
     a color picker. } }
}

\s { Hunt treasures } runs the search. The star chart opens as a new image
window. The result window shows the illustrated report; \s { Open HTML }
opens it in your web browser, and \s { Save HTML… } saves it to a file you
choose.

}

\subsection { Shared option: report attribution } {

A checkbox under the mode tabs, \s { Credit Sky Intruders at the end of
reports } , applies to both modes. It is off by default. See
\lref attribution { Report attribution } .

}

}

\section { \label network Network connections } {

\subsection { Hosts contacted } {

Every connection is made over HTTPS, through the networking of PixInsight
itself ( \c NetworkTransfer ). An address that does not start with
\c { https:// } is refused before any transfer, including a mirror address
entered by you. The script sets no proxy of its own: a proxy must be
configured in PixInsight. The script contacts the following hosts, and only
these:

\table [header] {
   { { Host } { When } { What is sent } }
   { { \c celestrak.org } { Night trails. } { The name of the orbital element
     group requested, for example \c active . The satellite catalog (SATCAT)
     is a plain file download. } }
   { { \c raw.githubusercontent.com/caelo-works/tle-mirror } { Night trails,
     when CelesTrak does not answer. } { The same group name. This is a mirror
     of CelesTrak data operated by CaeloWorks on GitHub, published with a
     SHA-256 manifest. It also provides the full catalog group, which only
     the mirror publishes, and a copy of the SATCAT. } }
   { { The mirror you enter } { Night trails, only when \s { Orbital data
     mirror URL: } is filled. } { The group name. Only an \c { https:// }
     address is accepted. } }
   { { \c vizier.cds.unistra.fr } { Treasure Hunt: VizieR at the CDS, for
     galaxies, quasars, planetary nebulae and bright stars. } { The field
     centre (RA/Dec), the search radius, the catalog and the columns wanted,
     and the row cap. } }
   { { \c ssp.imcce.fr } { Treasure Hunt: SkyBoT at the IMCCE, for
     asteroids. } { The field centre (RA/Dec), the search radius and the
     capture epoch. } }
}

}

\subsection { What is never sent } {

Your observer site is never sent: satellite orbits are computed on your
computer. File names, image data and identifiers of any kind are never sent
either. There is no telemetry and no usage tracking. Everything downloaded is
data, orbital elements and catalog rows; nothing downloaded is ever executed.

The script makes no automatic connection to the CaeloWorks website. The
\e { by CaeloWorks } label in the header of the dialog opens the product page,
\xref https://pixinsight-scripts.caelo.works/en/scripts/sky-intruders
{ pixinsight-scripts.caelo.works } , in your web browser only when you click
it (the French interface opens the French page).

}

\subsection { Data integrity } {

\list [spaced] {

{ \s { Orbital elements } , from every source: each element line carries a
modulo-10 checksum, verified on download. An element set that fails is
dropped, and the console says how many were dropped. A download in which more
than 5 \% of the element sets fail is rejected as corrupt, and the next
source, or the cache, is used instead. }

{ \s { CaeloWorks mirror } : every file taken from the mirror is checked
against its SHA-256 manifest, \c { tle/SHA256SUMS } . After one retry, a file
that still does not match is not used; if the manifest cannot be fetched, no
mirror file is used at all. CelesTrak or the cache is then used instead. }

{ \s { VizieR, SkyBoT and the CelesTrak SATCAT } publish no checksums. They
are protected by HTTPS and by strict format validation: an answer that is not
genuine catalog data, such as an error or maintenance page, is rejected,
reported, and never cached. }

}

}

}

\section { \label attribution Report attribution } {

The checkbox \s { Credit Sky Intruders at the end of reports } sits under the
mode tabs and is shared by both modes. Its tooltip reads: \e { Adds the line
“Report generated by Sky Intruders for PixInsight” and the product page
address at the end of the night log, its forum text and the Treasure Hunt HTML
report. Off by default: what you publish is up to you. }

The option is saved in \c settings.json as \c reportAttribution . It is
\c false by default, including for settings files written by older versions.
When it is off, no attribution line is added to any report format.

The \e { by CaeloWorks } label in the header of the dialog is not affected by
this option. It stays in the dialog, and opens the product page only when you
click it.

}

\section { External programs } {

Sky Intruders starts no program of its own, and no executable is shipped with
it. Only when you click \s { Open image } , \s { Open HTML } or the
\e { by CaeloWorks } label, it asks your operating system to open the file or
the address with its default application. It runs \c { cmd /c start } on
Windows, \c { /usr/bin/open } on macOS and \c { xdg-open } on Linux, with the
file path or the address as argument. Nothing is launched without an explicit
click from you.

}

\section { \label files Files written on your computer } {

\subsection { The data folder } {

Everything persistent is stored in one folder of your home directory
( \c { \%USERPROFILE\% } on Windows, \c { ~ } on macOS and Linux):
\c { .caeloworks/sky-intruders/ } . Nothing is written inside the PixInsight
installation.

\table [header] {
   { { Path } { Content } { Safe to delete? } }
   { { \c settings.json } { The dialog settings. } { Yes: every setting is
     reset to its default. } }
   { { \c history.json } { Your personal night records. } { Yes, but the
     records are lost for good. } }
   { { \c { tle/ } } { The orbital element and SATCAT cache. } { Yes: the data
     is downloaded again on the next run. } }
   { { \c { treasure-cache/ } } { The deep catalog answers, kept 30 days. }
     { Yes: the catalogs are queried again. } }
}

To stop keeping night records, close the script, open \c settings.json in a
text editor and set \c { "saveHistory": false } ; delete \c history.json if you
want the existing records gone too. The script rewrites \c settings.json when
its window closes, so edit it while the script is closed. To remove everything
the script stores, delete the whole \c { .caeloworks/sky-intruders } folder
while the script is closed.

}

\subsection { The temporary folder } {

The script also writes to the system temporary folder, as reported by
PixInsight:

\list [spaced] {

{ \c { si-night-reg-<token>/ } , the registered frames of a Night trails run:
removed at the end of every run. A leftover one means that PixInsight was
stopped during a run; it is safe to delete. }

{ \c { si-render-* } , image encoding scratch files: removed right after
use. }

{ The copy of the Treasure Hunt HTML report written by \s { Open HTML } , and
the optional debug overlays \c { si-frame-annotated-<token>-*.png } : removed
when the script window closes. }

{ \c { SkyIntruders-night-<date>-<token>.png } , the annotated night image:
\s { kept on purpose, and not cleaned up automatically } . It stays in the
system temporary folder because it is the image the result window opens. Save
it elsewhere if you want to keep it; the copy in the temporary folder can be
deleted at any time. }

}

Files you save yourself, with \s { Save report… } or \s { Save HTML… } , are
written only where you choose.

}

}

\section { Code signature } {

The entry script of the package is signed with the CaeloWorks Certified
PixInsight Developer identity; the signature is the \c SkyIntruders.xsgn file
installed next to it. The script therefore runs with the \e { Allow execution
of unsigned scripts } option of PixInsight turned off. Modifying the installed
files invalidates the signature.

}

